    kwargs[\"ax\"] = getattr(ax, \"left_ax\", ax)\n70 |     plot_obj = PLOT_CLASSES[kind](data, **kwargs)\n71 |     plot_obj.generate()\n72 |     plot_obj.draw()\n73 |     return plot_obj.result\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `plot` function in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/__init__.py` is responsible for initiating the plotting process. It creates an instance of the appropriate plotting class based on the `kind` argument and then calls the `generate` and `draw` methods on that instance.\n\nGiven that the issue is about grouping columns in subplots, we should also look at the `_get_axes_layout` and `_make_legend` functions in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` to see how the axes are arranged and how legends are handled.\n\nLet's examine the `_get_axes_layout` function next.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=line_nums>[968, 969, 970, 971, 972, 973, 974, 975, 976, 977]</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 968, 969, 970, 971, 972, 973, 974, 975, 976, 977\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._get_axes_layout`.\nSource: Retrieved code context including pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 968, 969, 970, 971, 972, 973, 974, 975, 976, 977.\n```\n968 | def _get_axes_layout(self) -> tuple[int, int]:\n969 |         axes = self._get_subplots()\n970 |         x_set = set()\n971 |         y_set = set()\n972 |         for ax in axes:\n973 |             # check axes coordinates to estimate layout\n974 |             points = ax.get_position().get_points()\n975 |             x_set.add(points[0][0])\n976 |             y_set.add(points[0][1])\n977 |         return (len(y_set), len(x_set))\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `_get_axes_layout` function determines the layout of the subplots by checking the coordinates of each subplot axis. This function will be important for adjusting how the subplots are arranged when implementing the subplot grouping feature.\n\nNext, let's examine the `_make_legend` function to see how legends are handled in the plotting process.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=line_nums>[650, 651, 652, 653, 654, 655, 656, 657, 658, 659, 660, 661, 662, 663, 664, 665, 666, 667, 668, 669, 670, 671, 672, 673, 674, 675, 676, 677, 678, 679, 680, 681]</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 650, 651, 652, 653, 654, 655, 656, 657, 658, 659, 660, 661, 662, 663, 664, 665, 666, 667, 668, 669, 670, 671, 672, 673, 674, 675, 676, 677, 678, 679, 680, 681\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._make_legend`.\nSource: Retrieved code context including pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 650, 651, 652, 653, 654, 655, 656, 657, 658, 659, 660, 661, 662, 663, 664, 665, 666, 667, 668, 669, 670, 671, 672, 673, 674, 675, 676, 677, 678, 679, 680, 681.\n```\n650 | def _make_legend(self) -> None:\n651 |         ax, leg = self._get_ax_legend(self.axes[0])\n652 | \n653 |         handles = []\n654 |         labels = []\n655 |         title = \"\"\n656 | \n657 |         if not self.subplots:\n658 |             if leg is not None:\n659 |                 title = leg.get_title().get_text()\n660 |                 # Replace leg.LegendHandles because it misses marker info\n661 |                 handles = leg.legendHandles\n662 |                 labels = [x.get_text() for x in leg.get_texts()]\n663 | \n664 |             if self.legend:\n665 |                 if self.legend == \"reverse\":\n666 |                     handles += reversed(self.legend_handles)\n667 |                     labels += reversed(self.legend_labels)\n668 |                 else:\n669 |                     handles += self.legend_handles\n670 |                     labels += self.legend_labels\n671 | \n672 |                 if self.legend_title is not None:\n673 |                     title = self.legend_title\n674 | \n675 |             if len(handles) > 0:\n676 |                 ax.legend(handles, labels, loc=\"best\", title=title)\n677 | \n678 |         elif self.subplots and self.legend:\n679 |             for ax in self.axes:\n680 |                 if ax.get_visible():\n681 |                     ax.legend(loc=\"best\")\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `_make_legend` function in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` handles the creation and placement of legends for plots, including those with subplots. Understanding how legends are managed can provide insights into how subplot configurations might need to be adjusted for grouping columns.\n\nGiven the context and the functions we've identified, let's now look at the `create_subplots` function in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py` to understand how subplots are initially created and how we might modify this to support grouping.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>[\"create_subplots\"]</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"create_subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `create_subplots`.\n```\n132 | def create_subplots(\n133 |     naxes: int,\n134 |     sharex: bool = False,\n135 |     sharey: bool = False,\n136 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n137 |     subplot_kw=None,\n138 |     ax=None,\n139 |     layout=None,\n140 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n141 |     **fig_kw,\n142 | ):\n143 |     \"\"\"\n144 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n145 | \n146 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n147 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n148 | \n149 |     Parameters\n150 |     ----------\n151 |     naxes : int\n152 |       Number of required axes. Exceeded axes are set invisible. Default is\n153 |       nrows * ncols.\n154 | \n155 |     sharex : bool\n156 |       If True, the X axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n157 | \n158 |     sharey : bool\n159 |       If True, the Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n160 | \n161 |     squeeze : bool\n162 | \n163 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n164 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n165 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n166 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n167 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n168 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n169 | \n170 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n171 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n172 | \n173 |     subplot_kw : dict\n174 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n175 |       subplots.\n176 | \n177 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n178 | \n179 |     layout : tuple\n180 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n181 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n182 | \n183 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n184 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n185 | \n186 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n187 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n188 |         automatically included here.\n189 | \n190 |     Returns\n191 |     -------\n192 |     fig, ax : tuple\n193 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n194 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n195 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n196 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n197 | \n198 |     Examples\n199 |     --------\n200 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n201 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n202 | \n203 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n204 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n205 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n206 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n207 | \n208 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n209 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n210 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n211 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n212 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n213 | \n214 |     # Four polar axes\n215 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n216 |     \"\"\"\n217 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n218 | \n219 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n220 |         subplot_kw = {}\n221 | \n222 |     if ax is None:\n223 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n224 |     else:\n225 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n226 |             if squeeze:\n227 |                 ax = flatten_axes(ax)\n228 |             if layout is not None:\n229 |                 warnings.warn(\n230 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n231 |                     UserWarning,\n232 |                 )\n233 |             if sharex or sharey:\n234 |                 warnings.warn(\n235 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n236 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n237 |                     UserWarning,\n238 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n239 |                 )\n240 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n241 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n242 |                 return fig, ax\n243 |             else:\n244 |                 raise ValueError(\n245 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n246 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n247 |                 )\n248 | \n249 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n250 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n251 |         if naxes == 1:\n252 |             if squeeze:\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 return fig, flatten_axes(ax)\n256 |         else:\n257 |             warnings.warn(\n258 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n259 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n260 |                 UserWarning,\n261 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n262 |             )\n263 |             fig.clear()\n264 | \n265 |     nrows, ncols = _get_layout(naxes, layout=layout, layout_type=layout_type)\n266 |     nplots = nrows * ncols\n267 | \n268 |     # Create empty object array to hold all axes.  It's easiest to make it 1-d\n269 |     # so we can just append subplots upon creation, and then\n270 |     axarr = np.empty(nplots, dtype=object)\n271 | \n272 |     # Create first subplot separately, so we can share it if requested\n273 |     ax0 = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, 1, **subplot_kw)\n274 | \n275 |     if sharex:\n276 |         subplot_kw[\"sharex\"] = ax0\n277 |     if sharey:\n278 |         subplot_kw[\"sharey\"] = ax0\n279 |     axarr[0] = ax0\n280 | \n281 |     # Note off-by-one counting because add_subplot uses the MATLAB 1-based\n282 |     # convention.\n283 |     for i in range(1, nplots):\n284 |         kwds = subplot_kw.copy()\n285 |         # Set sharex and sharey to None for blank/dummy axes, these can\n286 |         # interfere with proper axis limits on the visible axes if\n287 |         # they share axes e.g. issue #7528\n288 |         if i >= naxes:\n289 |             kwds[\"sharex\"] = None\n290 |             kwds[\"sharey\"] = None\n291 |         ax = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, i + 1, **kwds)\n292 |         axarr[i] = ax\n293 | \n294 |     if naxes != nplots:\n295 |         for ax in axarr[naxes:]:\n296 |             ax.set_visible(False)\n297 | \n298 |     handle_shared_axes(axarr, nplots, naxes, nrows, ncols, sharex, sharey)\n299 | \n300 |     if squeeze:\n301 |         # Reshape the array to have the final desired dimension (nrow,ncol),\n302 |         # though discarding unneeded dimensions that equal 1.  If we only have\n303 |         # one subplot, just return it instead of a 1-element array.\n304 |         if nplots == 1:\n305 |             axes = axarr[0]\n306 |         else:\n307 |             axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols).squeeze()\n308 |     else:\n309 |         # returned axis array will be always 2-d, even if nrows=ncols=1\n310 |         axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols)\n311 | \n312 |     return fig, axes\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `create_subplots` function in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py` is responsible for creating the subplots based on the number of axes required (`naxes`), sharing options (`sharex`, `sharey`), layout preferences, and other keyword arguments. This function will be crucial for modifying the subplot creation process to accommodate grouping columns on the same subplot.\n\nNow, let's identify potential places in the `pandas/core/indexes/base.py` file that might be relevant for handling the indices of the grouped columns.\n\nSince the issue involves grouping columns, the `Index.get_loc` and `Index.get_indexer` functions might be useful for determining the positions of the grouped columns.\n\nLet's search for these functions in `pandas/core/indexes/base.py`.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>[\"get_loc\", \"get_indexer\"]</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/core/indexes/base.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"get_loc\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index.get_loc`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `get_loc`.\n```\n3733 | def get_loc(self, key, method=None, tolerance=None):\n3734 |         \"\"\"\n3735 |         Get integer location, slice or boolean mask for requested label.\n3736 | \n3737 |         Parameters\n3738 |         ----------\n3739 |         key : label\n3740 |         method : {None, 'pad'/'ffill', 'backfill'/'bfill', 'nearest'}, optional\n3741 |             * default: exact matches only.\n3742 |             * pad / ffill: find the PREVIOUS index value if no exact match.\n3743 |             * backfill / bfill: use NEXT index value if no exact match\n3744 |             * nearest: use the NEAREST index value if no exact match. Tied\n3745 |               distances are broken by preferring the larger index value.\n3746 |         tolerance : int or float, optional\n3747 |             Maximum distance from index value for inexact matches. The value of\n3748 |             the index at the matching location must satisfy the equation\n3749 |             ``abs(index[loc] - key) <= tolerance``.\n3750 | \n3751 |         Returns\n3752 |         -------\n3753 |         loc : int if unique index, slice if monotonic index, else mask\n3754 | \n3755 |         Examples\n3756 |         --------\n3757 |         >>> unique_index = pd.Index(list('abc'))\n3758 |         >>> unique_index.get_loc('b')\n3759 |         1\n3760 | \n3761 |         >>> monotonic_index = pd.Index(list('abbc'))\n3762 |         >>> monotonic_index.get_loc('b')\n3763 |         slice(1, 3, None)\n3764 | \n3765 |         >>> non_monotonic_index = pd.Index(list('abcb'))\n3766 |         >>> non_monotonic_index.get_loc('b')\n3767 |         array([False,  True, False,  True])\n3768 |         \"\"\"\n3769 |         if method is None:\n3770 |             if tolerance is not None:\n3771 |                 raise ValueError(\n3772 |                     \"tolerance argument only valid if using pad, \"\n3773 |                     \"backfill or nearest lookups\"\n3774 |                 )\n3775 |             casted_key = self._maybe_cast_indexer(key)\n3776 |             try:\n3777 |                 return self._engine.get_loc(casted_key)\n3778 |             except KeyError as err:\n3779 |                 raise KeyError(key) from err\n3780 |             except TypeError:\n3781 |                 # If we have a listlike key, _check_indexing_error will raise\n3782 |                 #  InvalidIndexError. Otherwise we fall through and re-raise\n3783 |                 #  the TypeError.\n3784 |                 self._check_indexing_error(key)\n3785 |                 raise\n3786 | \n3787 |         # GH#42269\n3788 |         warnings.warn(\n3789 |             f\"Passing method to {type(self).__name__}.get_loc is deprecated \"\n3790 |             \"and will raise in a future version. Use \"\n3791 |             \"index.get_indexer([item], method=...) instead.\",\n3792 |             FutureWarning,\n3793 |             stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n3794 |         )\n3795 | \n3796 |         if is_scalar(key) and isna(key) and not self.hasnans:\n3797 |             raise KeyError(key)\n3798 | \n3799 |         if tolerance is not None:\n3800 |             tolerance = self._convert_tolerance(tolerance, np.asarray(key))\n3801 | \n3802 |         indexer = self.get_indexer([key], method=method, tolerance=tolerance)\n3803 |         if indexer.ndim > 1 or indexer.size > 1:\n3804 |             raise TypeError(\"get_loc requires scalar valued input\")\n3805 |         loc = indexer.item()\n3806 |         if loc == -1:\n3807 |             raise KeyError(key)\n3808 |         return loc\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"get_indexer\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index.get_indexer`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `get_indexer`.\n```\n3864 | def get_indexer(\n3865 |         self,\n3866 |         target,\n3867 |         method: str_t | None = None,\n3868 |         limit: int | None = None,\n3869 |         tolerance=None,\n3870 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n3871 |         method = missing.clean_reindex_fill_method(method)\n3872 |         orig_target = target\n3873 |         target = self._maybe_cast_listlike_indexer(target)\n3874 | \n3875 |         self._check_indexing_method(method, limit, tolerance)\n3876 | \n3877 |         if not self._index_as_unique:\n3878 |             raise InvalidIndexError(self._requires_unique_msg)\n3879 | \n3880 |         if len(target) == 0:\n3881 |             return np.array([], dtype=np.intp)\n3882 | \n3883 |         if not self._should_compare(target) and not self._should_partial_index(target):\n3884 |             # IntervalIndex get special treatment bc numeric scalars can be\n3885 |             #  matched to Interval scalars\n3886 |             return self._get_indexer_non_comparable(target, method=method, unique=True)\n3887 | \n3888 |         if is_categorical_dtype(self.dtype):\n3889 |             # _maybe_cast_listlike_indexer ensures target has our dtype\n3890 |             #  (could improve perf by doing _should_compare check earlier?)\n3891 |             assert is_dtype_equal(self.dtype, target.dtype)\n3892 | \n3893 |             indexer = self._engine.get_indexer(target.codes)\n3894 |             if self.hasnans and target.hasnans:\n3895 |                 # After _maybe_cast_listlike_indexer, target elements which do not\n3896 |                 # belong to some category are changed to NaNs\n3897 |                 # Mask to track actual NaN values compared to inserted NaN values\n3898 |                 # GH#45361\n3899 |                 target_nans = isna(orig_target)\n3900 |                 loc = self.get_loc(np.nan)\n3901 |                 mask = target.isna()\n3902 |                 indexer[target_nans] = loc\n3903 |                 indexer[mask & ~target_nans] = -1\n3904 |             return indexer\n3905 | \n3906 |         if is_categorical_dtype(target.dtype):\n3907 |             # potential fastpath\n3908 |             # get an indexer for unique categories then propagate to codes via take_nd\n3909 |             # get_indexer instead of _get_indexer needed for MultiIndex cases\n3910 |             #  e.g. test_append_different_columns_types\n3911 |             categories_indexer = self.get_indexer(target.categories)\n3912 | \n3913 |             indexer = algos.take_nd(categories_indexer, target.codes, fill_value=-1)\n3914 | \n3915 |             if (not self._is_multi and self.hasnans) and target.hasnans:\n3916 |                 # Exclude MultiIndex because hasnans raises NotImplementedError\n3917 |                 # we should only get here if we are unique, so loc is an integer\n3918 |                 # GH#41934\n3919 |                 loc = self.get_loc(np.nan)\n3920 |                 mask = target.isna()\n3921 |                 indexer[mask] = loc\n3922 | \n3923 |             return ensure_platform_int(indexer)\n3924 | \n3925 |         pself, ptarget = self._maybe_promote(target)\n3926 |         if pself is not self or ptarget is not target:\n3927 |             return pself.get_indexer(\n3928 |                 ptarget, method=method, limit=limit, tolerance=tolerance\n3929 |             )\n3930 | \n3931 |         if is_dtype_equal(self.dtype, target.dtype) and self.equals(target):\n3932 |             # Only call equals if we have same dtype to avoid inference/casting\n3933 |             return np.arange(len(target), dtype=np.intp)\n3934 | \n3935 |         if not is_dtype_equal(self.dtype, target.dtype) and not is_interval_dtype(\n3936 |             self.dtype\n3937 |         ):\n3938 |             # IntervalIndex gets special treatment for partial-indexing\n3939 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(target)\n3940 | \n3941 |             this = self.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3942 |             target = target.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3943 |             return this._get_indexer(\n3944 |                 target, method=method, limit=limit, tolerance=tolerance\n3945 |             )\n3946 | \n3947 |         return self._get_indexer(target, method, limit, tolerance)\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `get_loc` and `get_indexer` functions in `pandas/core/indexes/base.py` are used for indexing and finding the locations of labels within an index. While these functions are important for handling individual column indices, they are not directly related to the subplot grouping feature. Instead, we should focus on how subplots are created and managed.\n\nLet's summarize the key functions we've identified that are relevant to subplot handling and grouping:\n\n1. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/__init__.py:plot`\n   - Initiates the plotting process and creates the appropriate plotting object.\n\n2. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._setup_subplots`\n   - Sets up the subplots based on the number of series and layout parameters.\n\n3. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._adorn_subplots`\n   - Adorns subplots with titles and legends.\n\n4. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._make_legend`\n   - Handles the creation and placement of legends for plots.\n\n5. `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`\n   - Creates a figure with a set of subplots already made, handling layout and sharing options.\n\nGiven these functions, we can now outline the locations requiring modification for implementing the subplot grouping feature.\n\n```\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/__init__.py\nfunction: plot\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nfunction: _setup_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nfunction: _adorn_subplots\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py\nfunction: _make_legend\n\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py\nfunction: create_subplots\n```\n\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 364290, "completion_tokens": 2157}, "time": 313.04013419151306}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-60457", "found_files": [["pandas/io/formats/csvs.py", "pandas/io/formats/format.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/indexing.py:_LocIndexer", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:PlanePlot", "pandas/io/formats/format.py:FloatArrayFormatter", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameColumnApply", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:IntervalDtype", "pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index", "pandas/core/resample.py:Resampler", "pandas/core/arrays/datetimelike.py:DatetimeLikeArrayMixin"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/indexing.py:_LocIndexer._get_label", "pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:PlanePlot._get_nseries", "pandas/io/formats/format.py:FloatArrayFormatter._value_formatter", "pandas/core/apply.py:FrameColumnApply.result_columns", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:IntervalDtype.closed", "pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index.__copy__", "pandas/core/resample.py:Resampler.sem", "pandas/core/arrays/datetimelike.py:DatetimeLikeArrayMixin.__radd__", "pandas/io/formats/format.py:FloatArrayFormatter.get_result_as_array", "pandas/core/dtypes/dtypes.py:IntervalDtype.type"]], "raw_output_loc": ["pandas/io/formats/csvs.py\nclass: DataFrameFormatter\nfunction: to_csv\n\npandas/io/formats/format.py\nline: 1254\nline: 1259\nline: 1261\nline: 1262\nline: 1263\nline: 1264\nclass: FloatArrayFormatter\nfunction: __init__\n\npandas/io/formats/format.py\nline: 1273\nline: 1282\nline: 1284\nline: 1300\nline: 1306\nline: 1308\nline: 1309\nclass: FloatArrayFormatter\nfunction: _value_formatter\n\npandas/io/formats/format.py\nline: 1328\nline: 1340\nline: 1369\nline: 1370\nline: 1372\nline: 1375\nline: 1378\nline: 1408\nclass: FloatArrayFormatter\nfunction: get_result_as_array\n\npandas/io/formats/format.py\nline: 1379\nline: 1383\nline: 1387\nline: 1388\nline: 1391\nline: 1394\nline: 1396\nline: 1403\nclass: FloatArrayFormatter\nfunction: format_values_with"], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "844b3191bd45b95cbaae341048bf7f367f086f2f", "problem_statement": "DOC: Add examples for float_format in to_csv documentation\n### Pandas version checks\r\n\r\n- [X] I have checked that the issue still exists on the latest versions of the docs on `main` [here](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/)\r\n\r\n\r\n### Location of the documentation\r\n\r\nhttps://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_csv.html\r\n\r\n### Documentation problem\r\n\r\nThe float_format parameter in to_csv is explained but lacks examples. Users might struggle to understand how to apply this parameter effectively without concrete examples in the documentation.\r\n\r\n### Suggested fix for documentation\r\n\r\nI suggest adding examples for float_format to make the documentation more beginner-friendly. Examples could include:\r\n\r\n```\r\n# Format floats to two decimal places\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example1.csv\", float_format=\"%.2f\")\r\n\r\n# Use scientific notation\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example2.csv\", float_format=\"{:.2e}\".format)\r\n```\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/generic.py b/pandas/core/generic.py\nindex a6be17a654aa7..3a48cc8a66076 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/generic.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/generic.py\n@@ -3878,6 +3878,14 @@ def to_csv(\n         >>> import os  # doctest: +SKIP\n         >>> os.makedirs(\"folder/subfolder\", exist_ok=True)  # doctest: +SKIP\n         >>> df.to_csv(\"folder/subfolder/out.csv\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n+\n+        Format floats to two decimal places:\n+\n+        >>> df.to_csv(\"out1.csv\", float_format=\"%.2f\")  # doctest: +SKIP\n+\n+        Format floats using scientific notation:\n+\n+        >>> df.to_csv(\"out2.csv\", float_format=\"{{:.2e}}\".format)  # doctest: +SKIP\n         \"\"\"\n         df = self if isinstance(self, ABCDataFrame) else self.to_frame()\n \n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 13330, "completion_tokens": 337}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'pandas-dev' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: DOC: Add examples for float_format in to_csv documentation\n\n### Pandas version checks\r\n\r\n- [X] I have checked that the issue still exists on the latest versions of the docs on `main` [here](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/)\r\n\r\n\r\n### Location of the documentation\r\n\r\nhttps://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.to_csv.html\r\n\r\n### Documentation problem\r\n\r\nThe float_format parameter in to_csv is explained but lacks examples. Users might struggle to understand how to apply this parameter effectively without concrete examples in the documentation.\r\n\r\n### Suggested fix for documentation\r\n\r\nI suggest adding examples for float_format to make the documentation more beginner-friendly. Examples could include:\r\n\r\n```\r\n# Format floats to two decimal places\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example1.csv\", float_format=\"%.2f\")\r\n\r\n# Use scientific notation\r\ndf.to_csv(\"example2.csv\", float_format=\"{:.2e}\".format)\r\n```\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental context information:\n\nYou will receive supplemental context for the current task. Use it to better understand the issue and to localize the relevant code more accurately.\n\nTreat this context as additional evidence, not as the final answer.\n- It may contain noise.\n- It may be incomplete.\n- You should carefully analyze it and decide how to use it when searching for relevant code locations.\n\nDepending on the settings, the supplemental context may include:\n1. Unique artifacts\n2. Unique dependency tree\n\n## How to use this supplemental context\n- Use it to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself.\n\n## What the provided information means\nAssume the issue sent to you is a feature request requesting feature F.\n\n1. Unique artifacts\n- The unique artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis.\n- For the requested feature described in the issue text, two Python scripts, A.py and B.py, describing the same process are executed and traced.\n- The only difference is that A.py is executed with feature F enabled, while B.py is executed with the feature disabled.\n- By comparing the two tracing results, the overlapping part between the trace for A.py and B.py is subtracted.\n- The remaining artifacts are the unique artifacts that appear only in A.py.\n- These unique artifacts are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree\n- The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts.\n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts.\n- It can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\n\nHere is the information about the current task:\n1. Unique artifacts (obtained through Software Reconnaissance-style analysis):\n# Unique Artifacts\n\nUse these unique artifacts as supplemental hints, not as final answers.\n\n## Summary\n- Trace A role: `feature_positive`\n- Trace B role: `feature_negative`\n- Comparison: `trace_a_minus_trace_b`\n- Unique functions in JSON: `12`\n- Unique functions shown here: `12`\n- Target package filter: `pandas`\n\n## Related Files\n- `pandas/io/formats/format.py`: `9` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/dtypes/common.py`: `2` unique function(s)\n- `pandas/core/dtypes/missing.py`: `1` unique function(s)\n\n## Artifact Candidates\n- artifact: `pandas/io/formats/format.py:<listcomp>`\n  - module: `pandas.io.formats.format`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 1333`\n  - observed lines: `1333, 1334, 1335`\n  - trace activity: 126 events, 2 calls, 122 line events, 2 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 15\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 1333`: `[`\n    - `line 1334`: `formatter(val) if not m else na_rep`\n    - `line 1335`: `for val, m in zip(values.ravel(), mask.ravel())`\n- artifact: `pandas/io/formats/format.py:base_formatter`\n  - module: `pandas.io.formats.format`\n  - first obse